Negative control — the chosen ADDRESS TYPE reaches the wire (AC-22, fix 8e).

Restores the pre-conversion hardcode in `mapIAddressData`: `type: data.type`
becomes `type: 1`, complete with the comment the original carried. The `type`
control still exists on the schema, the consumer can still pick Home / Office /
Holiday / Company, the model still records the choice — it is simply discarded
at the mapper, one step before the body is serialised. Right shape, no
capability.

Declared AC — AC-22: "a client picks an address type when editing" — set
`type: 3` on an existing address and assert the captured PUT body carries
`type: 3`. Asserting the schema exposes the four options does NOT discriminate;
under this mutant it still does.

Collateral RED, measured, and NOT this control's declared subject:
  - AC-24 — the create read-back. `mapIAddressData` is the CREATE body mapper
    as well as the full-payload update mapper, so a hardcoded type corrupts the
    POST body by the same edit.

AC-24's own control is `client-address.manager-create-amputation.must-fail.patch`,
which amputates the create limb instead. A red confined to the AC-22 read-back
means the type was dropped in mapping; a red in AC-24 alone means the limb is
gone.

